Z||000004||DecadenceR||08-01-2001||03:08 AM||||Is yours an 84 as well? I dont remember.<BR>Anyway, if it is, what I did to fix my cooling fas was tahe the WHOLE fan, shroud and all, from a later model. I even took about a foot of wiring that goes to the plug.<BR>Then, I spliced the 2 wires off the old wiring onto the new single wire, and I had to run my own ground to the body.<P>BEFORE you do that tho, get a volt meter.<BR>Take the negative lead and stick it into the black lead for the windshield wiper fluid motor (I say this becuse it is a ground and it holds itself pretty well) unplug the connector to the fan, and turn on your AC. <BR>Check both sides to see if you have power to ONE of the sides. (the other only has power when the temp is high enough) <BR>If you do and your fan doesn't work, it'e wither the motor or the resistor, which is what I had. if you DON'T get any power, then you have to check the relay that is located in front of the driver's side headlight ($60). Also on the 4cyl 84, there's the switch located in the head on the drivers side facing the front fire wall. (the one on top is the temp sensor) and that coudl also be bad, that's only about $10 if i rememebr right.<BR>If you have any question about my rambling, since it is late, IM me on AIM and I'll try to explain better or answer any Q.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-01-2001||03:08 AM||||Fierobaby,<P>To check all the cooling circuit start car and turn on A/C, radiator fan should come on. I it does come on your temp sensor for the fan is bad. If it doesn't come on replace fan relay after checking the fan motor for function. Any ?'s, ask them! <P>What's the problem with the brakes?<P>I just pulled out my map to see where Berea was located! If you had posted your problems here before I left and I didn't see them, I'm sorry. I just got home 3 weeks ago from Cleveland and even stopped in Parma to visit some friends!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DreXteR||08-01-2001||10:22 AM||||Well, I did something dif.  Because I leave in Puerto Rico(Always is summer time), just reroute the cables bypassing the fan relay and plug in directly. So when I turn on the car the fan will be on too and keep on until i turn off the car. Maybe this wont help you but is an option, here always is hot -its a good to keep the engine cool all the time. BTW also I take off the termostat. The lowest temp here in winter is 78*.<P><BR>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||08-01-2001||10:52 AM||||I hope this comes out right<P> one of the aspects of owning a sports car is they are not always all there when you need them - ask any MG owner<P> granted the Fiero is suppose to be an everyday driver, not some fussy touchy thing from england or italy<P>  but if you can separate out the fun and sporty aspects of driving your Fiero, from your need for daily transportation, then the negative emotions towards it will go away.<P>  Im not saying you cant have a car thats fun to drive and reliable too - you can, but it will cost you $$$.<P>  One of the sure signs that you own a sports car is the Love/Hate relationship that ensues.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroLisa||08-01-2001||10:56 AM||||Fierobaby: I warned you!!!! But you didn't listen  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>Of course there are going to be problems with the car, no matter if there were none on the day you bought it. It is a 16 or so yr old car. 5 weeks ago my fan went out, that was an easy fix, replaced the fan motor.  4 weeks ago, my catalytic converter clogged up, my overflow tank cracked, i finally got around to fixing my door locks, and found that my oil pan has a slight leak.  Last week my muffler finally sh*t out on me. THIS week my brakes are crap, but I'm thinking that as long as I use the right brake fluid  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> that problem should be solved.  That's the FUN in owning an older sports car- fixing it up!!!!!!  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierobaby||08-01-2001||12:00 PM||||Steve: Yes it is an 84 and that sounds like a good idea my dad has a volt meter... I printed out your response and once brakes are in and all (not to much longer... we are going to give that a shot!<P>Indiana: I also printed out what ytou said and darn you next time you come out let me know we can meet up!<P>dexter: wow I would die in that heat!<P>Ken: thanks for that pep talk you are correct I suppose I got myself into this!<P>lisa: before you do anything with the brake fluids get your skinny butt under that car and check those lines... we thought it was the brakes not the lines that were the problem in my car but as my father was driving him from e check the brakes went completly he thank god made it home ok. turns out the lines split...but still check them...<p>[This message has been edited by fierobaby (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Leadfoot||08-01-2001||02:25 PM||||I bought my car for $900 and spent 4 times as much repairing it (it doesn't help any if you beat the h*ll out of it daily).  Fierolisa is right, what do you expect from a 16 year old car.  The way I see it you should just sell it and look for one that is either newer or had all the major repairs done.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||08-01-2001||03:19 PM||||FIERO- you bet I'll let you know -BABY! !! !!<BR>LOL||||reg||1
Z||000012||DecadenceR||08-01-2001||03:41 PM||||I spent $300 on my Fiero. But I have put in ober $1000 in the past 6 months, and that's parts only. I have spilled more of my own blood on that car than I care to tally. One of the previous owners liked to cut wires, so I'm always finding stuff cut, and splicing it back together... But I LOVE working on my cars!!! I've had newer cars that didn't need anything except to clean and wax. They were fun to drive, but that's it.<BR>When I finally decide I want something more than an 84 Fiero, I'll know that whoever is lucky enough to get my car is buying a piece of ME... My ideas, my work, and my heart & dedication. You gotta love it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||maryjane||08-01-2001||06:23 PM||||Jane & I go thru the same problem every time something comes up with our 84. Do we put more $ in it or put that $ + what we can get for the Fiero toward a new car/truck. From '96 to 2000 it was little things(ign parts, water pmp,etc) in the last yr&1/2 it's been eng overhaul,front & rear brake job, paint job, large scratch on fender & broken qtr pnl(after the paint job of course). We get disgusted but still like the car a lot. As long as there is a love/hate deal, your ok.It's natural. If it goes for a long time of just a hate relationship, sell it & get something you would be happier with.Kinda like a relationship with  person I guess<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 2.5<BR>not slow not fast-just halfast||||reg||1
Z||000014||fierobaby||08-02-2001||01:19 AM||||well guys looks like this brake job is going to be a thosand times harder...I am now stuck doing it myself... I know nothing about cars...I'm not sure how my father expects me to do it...actually I think he expects me to come crawling back to him for help...but they way he is hes not getting it...so onceI figure out how far hes gotten and how much more I need to do I will let you know... then maybe you guys can help me... I have the Chiltons manual sitting in my room and I have been looking through it but its all like forigne to me!!! AHHHHHHHH!!!!!!!!! so I don't know what I am going to!<BR><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="LETTER"> AIM: Fierobaby87<BR>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero </FONT><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fast85GT||08-02-2001||04:10 PM||||I don't like the sensor for the fan.  If you look on the engine (fron on a V6), find the sensor that tells the fan when to turn on.  On my V6, it is a one wire setup.  If you ground it, it will turn on.  I just hard-wired mine to a secure bolt.  It runs all the time.<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||stsmithgt||08-02-2001||05:20 PM||||I know the feeling about wanting to scream when something goes wrong with your car.  I thought my clutch troubles were done after spending "tons" of money on a special clutch.  About your brakes.  Having a friend help even if they don't know much about cars either could help by reading your shop manual and assist in figuring out what it's telling you to do.  <I>The ol'"two heads are better than one" routine.</I><BR>I have to much back pain to be able to work on my car or at this time so my car is in a shop up in Sacramento, where it stranded me, and I'm back home 3 hours south of there.  I <I>thought</I> everything was ready to roll for several thousand miles without a clutch problem. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;<B>  NOT!!!!!!</B><BR>When the clutch pedal stops a third short of the required travel and gears grind and clunking metal along with scraping metal happens, you know then that the clutch needs even more #$@$*&*&^MONEY!  When you think you've replaced everything possible to keep it on the road, it will break or fail sooner than you had hoped.  They are old cars, so not only predictable parts will fail, but unpredictable.  For instance, I think my clutch fork is the culprit this time.  I hope it hasn't damaged anything else though my slave cylinder over extended letting fluid leak out.  This brings on deep thought of options to reduce frustrating levels of anger.<BR>* Sell it, is the simple solution.(You'll miss it for sure) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>* Have another vehicle to drive while it's down helps. (affording more than one vehicle can be tough)<BR>*Don't drive it as much when it is fixed. (this won't work when you love to drive it.) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>*Have a buddy do all the work or a "boy or girlfriend" to do it.  They might even pay for the work done. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>What to do, what to do????<BR>More than one person on this forum has pondered this cituation.<P>Sorry about the venting, but I feel your pain trying to keep your car in top shape. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||9
Z||000017||Fast85GT||08-02-2001||05:26 PM||||Make sure that you replace the obviuos, like the air cleaner, coolant, radiator lids, etc.  Also, especially if you have a MPFI system (multi-port fuel injection), replace a lot of vacuum hoses.  They can cause trouble if they are old, and you only have about 20,000 of them on that type of engine.  Look into replacing the starter and water pump if there are very many miles on it.<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||08-02-2001||06:53 PM||||Oh...and don't throw away any of the old parts, until you're absolutely sure the corresponding new part's an improvement!  Always better to be able to go back a step, if necessary. ||||reg||4
Z||000019||maryjane||08-02-2001||08:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>Oh...and don't throw away any of the old parts, until you're absolutely sure the corresponding new part's an improvement!  Always better to be able to go back a step, if necessary. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmm, Throw away a Fiero part. I've heard of that, always thought it was just another myth, like the farmers barn full of Harleys and Indians. Do some people really do it? Don't tell my better half, I've convinced her it is one of the Seven Deadly Sins <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||atarian||08-10-2001||05:27 AM||||Sorry no tips, but some advise. Having a fiero is like being in a love hate relationship. When you purchase one it is for better or worse. Just have plenty of money for a tow (when needed) and always have a backup car.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula88||08-10-2001||09:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>That's the FUN in owning an older sports car- fixing it up!!!!!!  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lisa, you are so right!  Any car that's 10-15 years old is going to need work as things just wear out.  Working on the car is part of the fun of owning it.  Otherwise, you might as well buy a new Civic and be done with it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Funny thing is, Pat tells me he's worked on my car more than his!  I guess that's the benefit of having a 62K mile original car.  'bout the only thing he's ever had to do to his was the clutch (twice).<P>Let me know how you like that new SpinTech muffler.  Do you think you'll have it in time for the next Tarheel Fieros meeting?  I'd love to hear it before you head back off to school.||||reg||6
Z||000022||FieroLisa||08-10-2001||11:47 AM||||John I HOPE I have my new Spintech on before I go to school- my muffler rattle is not so attractive. It's supposed to get here in 4 days...||||reg||1
